u've seen, and they went out and they spoke to the schools, and they got a very good reaction. And his mother was actually there, and she spoke. And she told some of the stories that really had not come out before. The only person who really talked about the Holocaust and the war was George's uncle Ernest, because he was still active in the military even though he was retired, but he did talk about it. They got very good feedback. You know, there were articles in the Inquirer, the Jewish Exponent about that. And different school districts asked them to come out and speak. So our rabbi who was Rabbi Addison at the time, and George and the group of people went out to the community and talked to these people about what had happened, what their memories were. And that continued for a while. And the thought came to George that perhaps we should have a memorial. We had been to different synagogues around the city for weddings and bar mitzvahs and different events. And so the committee, which I was a part of, went to the different synagogues, saw their memorials. And it was decided that the memorial we should have should be to the children, because other than the butterfly symbol that we have and some of the songs and poems that they wrote, there really is very little memory of them. And we decided to call it "Remember Us." You know, "Please come and remember us," because anything you talk about is a memory. And the deniers can no longer say, "This didn't happen." So, we actually had big fundraisers. And we raised quite a bit of money for the Holocaust memorial. You've seen the picture. It is beautiful. It centers in on the children. There are children's little shoes. There's a bike. It shows the barbed wire. And I will honestly say it was the women on the committee who had honed in on the children's portion. And it's sitting in our lobby. When you come into our synagogue [GESTURING] and look to the left, it's right there. And for everybody to see and to remember. Well, th